{"text":"[HSTU (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17152) supports heterogeneous feature representations within sequential transduction, providing a basis for adding content while retaining its backbone. [HLLM (2024)](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.12740v1) jointly optimizes an item-text encoder and a user-sequence encoder through next-item prediction; it explicitly evaluates training both from scratch, although pretrained initialization performs better. Transferable idea: compress each title/description into one learned item vector before sequential modeling. HLLM itself uses a different backbone, so its architecture and reported gains cannot be directly attributed to HSTU.

[AttrFormer (KDD 2025)](https://www.amazon.science/publications/learning-attribute-as-explicit-relation-for-sequential-recommendation) models attributes as explicit item relations rather than relying entirely on blended ID/attribute embeddings. A task-compatible hypothesis is to derive lexical tokens only from supplied titles/descriptions—model numbers, connector names, dimensions, format markers—and learn their embeddings jointly with item IDs inside one HSTU network. A small randomly initialized text encoder, field embeddings, and a learned residual gate could supply shared input/candidate representations under one next-item loss. These are proposed adaptations, not established HSTU results; matching lexical attributes does not establish actual product compatibility.

Many recent text recommenders violate the requested initialization constraints: [ITS-Rec (2025)](https://dspace.hansung.ac.kr/bitstream/2024.oak/10390/2/AR_D2A1A2_ITS-Rec_%20A%20Sequential%20Recommendation%20Model%20Using%20Item%20Textual%20Information.pdf) uses pretrained BERT and reviews. The strongest applicable direction is therefore a compact, jointly trained ID-plus-text HSTU, with all candidate scores produced by that single network. The retrieved evidence does not establish a directly matching 2026 method or guarantee validation gains."}
